  "solutions": [
    {"ordinal": 0, "title": "Grow Each Window Until It Breaks", "intuition_md": "Fix where a stretch starts and grow it one reading at a time, tracking its running min and max. The moment the spread passes `k`, no longer stretch from this start can be calm either, because adding readings can only widen the spread. So stop and try the next start.\n\nSimple and obviously correct, but when the whole input is calm nothing ever breaks early, and every start scans to the end.", "algorithm_md": "For each `start`, set `lo = hi = readings[start]`. For each `end` from `start` on, update `lo`/`hi`. If `hi - lo > k`, break; otherwise record `end - start + 1`.", "code": "def longest_calm_stretch(readings: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    best = 0\n    for start in range(len(readings)):\n        lo = hi = readings[start]\n        for end in range(start, len(readings)):\n            lo = min(lo, readings[end])\n            hi = max(hi, readings[end])\n            if hi - lo > k:\n                break  # growing the window can only widen the spread\n            best = max(best, end - start + 1)\n    return best\n", "time_complexity": "O(n^2)", "time_complexity_reason": "Up to $n$ starts, each scanning up to $n$ readings. The early break helps on noisy data but not in the worst case (an all-calm input).", "space_complexity": "O(1)", "space_complexity_reason": "Only the running `lo`, `hi` and `best`."},
    {"ordinal": 1, "title": "Sliding Window with Monotonic Deques", "intuition_md": "Slide one window instead of restarting: extend the right edge every step, and advance the left edge only as far as needed to make the window calm again. Both edges only move forward, so the total work is linear, provided the window's min and max are available in $O(1)$ as it slides.\n\nThat's what a **monotonic deque** gives. Keep indices whose values *increase* from front to back: a new reading evicts every larger value behind it, since those can never be the minimum again while the new one is in the window. The front is then always the window's minimum. A second, decreasing deque tracks the maximum. When the left edge passes a front index, that index leaves its deque.", "algorithm_md": "1. For each `end`: pop from the back of `lows` every index whose value is `>= readings[end]`, then append `end`. Do the mirror image for `highs` with `<=`.\n2. While `readings[highs[0]] - readings[lows[0]] > k`: advance `start`, and pop the front of each deque if it now lies left of `start`.\n3. Record `end - start + 1`.", "code": "from collections import deque\n\n\ndef longest_calm_stretch(readings: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    lows = deque()   # indices whose values increase: lows[0] is the window's min\n    highs = deque()  # indices whose values decrease: highs[0] is the window's max\n    best = start = 0\n    for end, value in enumerate(readings):\n        while lows and readings[lows[-1]] >= value:\n            lows.pop()\n        lows.append(end)\n        while highs and readings[highs[-1]] <= value:\n            highs.pop()\n        highs.append(end)\n        # Shrink from the left until the window is calm again.\n        while readings[highs[0]] - readings[lows[0]] > k:\n            start += 1\n            if lows[0] < start:\n                lows.popleft()\n            if highs[0] < start:\n                highs.popleft()\n        best = max(best, end - start + 1)\n    return best\n", "time_complexity": "O(n)", "time_complexity_reason": "Each index is appended to and popped from each deque at most once, and `start` advances at most $n$ times in total.", "space_complexity": "O(n)", "space_complexity_reason": "In the worst case (strictly increasing readings) `lows` holds every index of the window."}
  ]
}
